Your concern is valid: reciprocal-rank fusion discards BM25 score strength. Since code and markdown are indexed separately, their raw BM25 scores also aren’t directly comparable: each index has its own document frequencies and score distribution. Simply adding the raw scores could let one index dominate for reasons unrelated to relevance.

I’d compare calibrated per-index scores, then combine them with weights for code and documentation. The weights should be tuned against the project’s answered-question evaluation data, rather than chosen by guesswork.

Proposed changes

Replace reciprocal-rank fusion with per-index BM25 score normalization, followed by a weighted combination of code and markdown scores.
Make the code/documentation weights explicit and tune them using the existing evaluation data.
Fix the k handling so an unfiltered query doesn’t pass k to an individual index that contains fewer than k chunks. The current merge test already reproduces this failure.
Remove the debug print of internal chunk indexes.
Add focused tests for score combination, k smaller than one index, and preserving CONTEXT=code / CONTEXT=markdown behavior.
I’d leave the score-normalization method and weight defaults open until we agree on the approach. Does this plan match what you want?

